abstract: >-
  A research knowledge base, reproducible experimentation environment and
  scholarly portfolio for artificial intelligence applied to wireless
  communication systems, with an emphasis on critical networks in which
  communication performance, availability, security and recovery behaviour
  affect safety, continuity of service or cyber-physical operation. The
  portfolio is organised around service assurance rather than average network
  performance: whether a critical service meets its deadline with a stated
  probability, and what it costs to keep that guarantee under faults,
  degradation, congestion, outages and adversarial activity. Three exploratory
  directions share one experimental substrate and are maintained as
  alternatives until evidence justifies narrowing: assurance-constrained
  multi-RAT intelligence, digital-twin-assisted self-healing, and cyber-
  resilient deterministic communications. Experiments are treated as
  evidence-bearing artefacts that declare their question, model, assumptions,
  execution procedure, metrics, limitations and reproducibility status, and
  the repository distinguishes explicitly between implemented, simulated,
  planned and unsupported capabilities. All scenarios are synthetic and all
  thresholds nominal and uncalibrated unless a calibration procedure is
  documented. This record documents research evolution; it is not a thesis
  proposal, an institutional commitment or a validated critical-infrastructure
  solution.
